2. PURL — Programmable URLs / Scroll

Repository:

https://github.com/akadaan310/purl

PURL is the programmable/addressable computational layer.

The important idea has evolved beyond simply:

«"A URL that represents a resource."»

The research direction is:

«Programmable URLs → programmable operations → programmable programmability.»

A URL can address a computational object, operation, program, artifact, or traversable state.

Agents should be able to discover computational operations through URLs.

They should be able to construct operations.

They should be able to compose operations.

They should be able to create reusable programs.

They should be able to name those programs.

They should be able to evolve them.

Those persisted computational artifacts are called:

Scrolls

A Scroll is a structured, versioned computational artifact.

Conceptually:

Scroll
├── identity
├── version
├── parent
├── purpose
├── inputs
├── symbols
├── operations
├── composition
├── dependencies
├── aliases
├── permissions
├── provenance
├── execution history
└── checkpoints

A Scroll should not be merely text.

It is a traversable computational object.

Example:

Scroll v1
   ↓ execution
observation
   ↓
Scroll v2
   ↓
execution
   ↓
Scroll v3

Versions should remain historically distinguishable.

Aliases should be resolvable.

Compositions should be observable.

Eventually recurring symbols, operation sequences, aliases, shorthand, and compositions may reveal an emergent computational language.

Do NOT prescribe that language prematurely.

Let the language emerge from use.

